{
  "requirement": "In every project, the test generated by `habeas setup` MUST run validation in an optimized process outside the test suite’s coverage instrumentation. Existing tests calling `habeas::validate()` MUST get this behavior when the dependency is updated. Ordinary `cargo test` MUST work without a separately installed CLI. Under `habeas coverage`, the test MUST reuse the running release CLI when it matches the Habeas dependency. Both paths MUST check the consumer project and propagate validation failures to the test.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests cover each obligation in `requirements.integration.validate.optimized`.\n\n- **Generated test calls `habeas::validate()`.** `tests/setup.rs#fn[setup_is_repeatable]` asserts that `tests/habeas.rs` is exactly `#[test] fn habeas() { habeas::validate(); }`.\n- **Existing tests get the behavior when the dependency updates.** The behavior lives inside `habeas::validate()` itself. Every validate test reaches it through the `validate_child` test, which calls `habeas::validate()`.\n- **Optimized process, outside the suite's coverage instrumentation.** `tests/validate.rs#fn[standalone_test_builds_the_dependency_validator_for_the_consumer]` uses a stub cargo to record its arguments. It asserts `--release`, `--manifest-path <dependency>/Cargo.toml`, `--target-dir`, and a trailing `-- validate`. The validator therefore runs as a separate release-built process.\n- **Ordinary `cargo test` needs no separately installed CLI.** The same standalone test offers a mismatched `HABEAS_RELEASE_VALIDATOR` and `HABEAS_VALIDATOR_SOURCE`, so the fallback is taken. The dependency's own cargo build supplies the validator.\n- **Under `habeas coverage`, the test reuses the running release CLI when it matches.**\n  - `tests/validate.rs#fn[matching_collector_validates_the_consumer_without_cargo]` sets a matching source and a stub validator. `CARGO` points at a path that must not be invoked, and the stub receives only `validate`.\n  - `src/execution_coverage/runner.rs#mod[tests]/fn[only_release_collectors_offer_themselves_to_test_processes]` covers the offering side. A release collector sets both variables to `current_exe` and `VALIDATOR_SOURCE`. A non-release collector sets neither.\n- **Both paths check the consumer project.** Both stub tests assert that the working directory is the consumer root. `tests/validate.rs#fn[validate_fails_the_test_with_every_error_and_authoring_instructions]` starts from a nested directory and runs the real validator against the project.\n- **Both paths propagate validation failures.**\n  - The standalone test and the matching-collector test each have a stub fail with \"consumer evidence is stale\". Each asserts that the test process exits non-zero and that stderr carries that message.\n  - The end-to-end test asserts failure, with the errors and authoring instructions in the output, for broken evidence. It asserts success for valid evidence.\n\nI found no requirement-violating behavior that these tests would accept.",
  "references": "ec691c667eb2c784bc1c42d5b767caeda000a69c6fdd9877b5d1fd3a34556e5c",
